3. Display Wiring (Cat 6 cable)
nred (display) = green (cat 6)
black (display) = green/white
green (data into display) = orange
yellow (data from display) = orange/white
blue = not connected

4. Programmer Wiring (only needed when setting up or for tuning)

yellow (5V) = brown (cat 6)
green (gnd) = green/white, shared with the display ground (cat 6)
purple (clk) = blue (cat 6)
blue (swim) = brown/white (cat 6)

5. Throttle Wiring

black/red/green on controller goes to black/red/green on hand throttle for initial testing. Then, connect to the draw wire encoder to actually sense the throttle ont he go kart. We will need to recalibrate the throttle positions when moving to the draw wire encoder so that we have:
0% throttle = full regen
30% throttle = no torque
100% throttle = 100% torque

6. Power Wiring

Large red/black on controller goes to battery

"E-Lock" on controlelr also goes to battery red. This is the axillary supply to the controller to actually turn it on.

"Hard-boot" si the only other connection. Leave it open for anticlockwise spinning, closed for clockwise spinning



Final Notes:

- The motor has been agressively tuned for maximum performance and throttle response, but is on a knife edge for stability. It is likely once it is bolted to the axle, the load/intertia/mechanical resonance will change the system from free wheeling on the bench, so we will need to retune on the kart itself.

- The motor is rated to 1.5kW at 60V. We have a nominal 72V battery, that float charges at 84V. The controller is rated for 3kW. So the motor is the weak link. It should be able to tolerate short term overloads, but will overheat fairly quickly at sustained full throttle (say 10 seconds from cold, 5 from warm?). So be careful with your lead foot.

- There are 3 types fo fire extinguisher worth considering
    - F500 are for lithium battery fires. Expensive, and probbaly unnecessary
    - ABE powder good general purpose option. Won't extingush the battery directly, but good at stopping it spreading. Highly corrosive, so will damage the go kart
    - Water. Good low cost option. Useless on the battery itself, but can be sprayed on nearby grass to stop it spreading.

We should try to have at least one 4.5kg ABE on hand, and a heap of water. If the battery goes up it will explode, then burn for a while then eventually just go out. So main priorities are preventing any injury from the initial explosion, then stoppign anythign spreading while it burns out. Most problems with lithium are from people wasting their extinguisher on the battery itself and not having anything to stop the subsequent grass fire when the flames take over again.
